main_claim:
Section 18 completes the low-mode case:
- late odd roles are assigned injectively by conditional samplers (clock lemma) against product reference transitions using masks and sketches;
- the history-adaptive prefix-moment requirement is transferred to fresh initial data by an erasure plus bounded-response-protocol plus survival-tilt argument (Lemma late-prefix-transfer);
- terminal LLL conditioning makes all late bad probabilities small while keeping 1+o(1) comparison;
- joint endpoint kernel bounds then give simultaneous Hall matchings in all low-mode palettes. This excludes the low modes and finishes R(Q_n)=O(2^n).

sibling_notes:
Several inequalities in the block need C_n->infinity (Sec. 2) to absorb fixed constants. This holds, but at an unspecified rate: the conflict bound 8K^2C_n^{-1}... <= e^4, sum_j e_{w,j}=o(1), and the Hall large-set exponent. Line 85-87 argues boundedness of h_i e^{-beta s_i} only for cluster mode. Direct and bounded modes rely on h_i=0 (Sec. 14 l.229: no internal mechanism), which the block never states but which is true. Things the block takes on faith and that Sec. 16/17/3 reviewers should confirm:
- the masked-list clause of S_v at |J|<=(log n)^4;
- E sigma_v(x) <= K/M_i (Lemma fresh-internal-prior);
- the uniform pair second moment with exponent 2d (end of Sec. 17);
- Prop initial-resampling-comparison's target-cell limit n^{10(A_c+10)};
- the clock lemma being valid with its size parameter replaced by n_clock=e^{n^{delta'}}.
